<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">ARBED</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Aciéries Réunies de Burbach-Eich-Dudelange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ARBED was a Luxembourg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> company formed in 1911 by the merger of several ironworks and steelworks. Using the minette iron ore of southern Luxembourg, it became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading steel producers and the country's largest employer, with plants in Luxembourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In 2002 it merged with Usinor and Aceralia to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelor">Arcelor</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Iron ore was discovered in Luxembourg in the 1850s and metallurgy introduced in 1876, and in the two decades before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> large integrated steelworks raised the country's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> output to 1,115,004 tonnes in 1913, when steel-making accounted for around 60% of industrial employment. In 1911 an extraordinary general meeting of the Société anonyme des Hauts Fourneaux et Forges de Dudelange, founded in 1882, decided to absorb the Société anonyme des Mines du Luxembourg et Forges de Sarrebruck and the Forges d'Eich, Le Gallais, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> et Cie. The new firm began with 21 blast furnaces, 3 electric furnaces, 2 steelmaking plants and several rolling mills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When German capital was withdrawn after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, ARBED and French and Belgian partners set up the Terres Rouges companies to buy the plants and mines west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had to sell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>; ARBED merged with the Société Metallurgique des Terres Rouges in 1937. In 1921 it founded the Companhia Siderúrgica Belgo-Mineira at Sabará, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, with Luxembourg absorbed into Germany, the company was renamed Hüttenwerke Burbach-Eich-Dudelingen and used several hundred forced labourers and prisoners of war from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In the three decades after 1945 national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> output rose from 2.45 million tonnes in 1950 to 6.45 million tonnes in 1974, when the steelworks and iron mines employed around 25,000 people. ARBED joined the SIDMAR complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1962. The crisis that followed the 1973 oil shock brought production down to 3.2 million tonnes by 1983, and in 1982 and 1983 the government invested heavily in the company, finally holding 42.9% of its shares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the 1990s ARBED turned towards long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> products and electric arc furnace steel abroad, founding Stahlwerk Thüringen in 1992, taking a majority in Klöckner Stahl in 1995 and forming a partnership with Aceralia in 1997. The last Luxembourg blast furnace, at Belval, stopped in 1997. The merger of 2002 created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelor">Arcelor</a>, which Mittal Steel took over in 2006 to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelormittal">ArcelorMittal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ARBED” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Thomas-Gilchrist process of 1879 made such ore usable for steel, blast furnaces and steelworks multiplied, Italian and other foreign workers arrived, and in 1911 several firms merged into <b>ARBED</b>, which became one of Europe’s leading steel producers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
